<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siemens</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1847">1847</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Siemens &amp; Halske</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Siemens is a German engineering company founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> in 1847 by Werner von Siemens and Johann Georg Halske as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> manufacturer. It built telegraph lines across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and expanded into electric power, lighting and railways, becoming one of the largest electrical firms in the world. It remains one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s leading industrial companies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/siemens.jpg" alt="Siemens" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Martin Falbisoner · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Wings,_Siemens_HQ_Munich,_April_2017.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
